    "survivors": "Fort William First Nation Health Services:\nElders Assistance Program: This program supports the well-being of Elders with various activities, including 55+ programs, and provides assistance for medical supplies and equipment like orthotics, custom footwear, and wheelchairs.\nHealth – Fort William First Nation",
    "youth": "-Fort William First Nation Health Services: Recreational Program: The Band Office has a recreational program, though specific physical activities may vary. You can contact them for current offerings\nHealth – Fort William First Nation \n\nYouth & Social Development – Fort William First Nation",
    "connect": "-Cultural Knowledge Transfer Programs: The community hosts programs where Elders and Knowledge Keepers lead sessions on traditional skills, history, and the Anishinaabe language. Activities include plant identification, fire-starting with flint and steel, and birch bark canoe building, ensuring that ancestral skills are passed down to the younger generation.\n\nLand-Based Learning: Activities often take place on the land (Anemki Wajiw/Mount McKay and the Lake Superior shores), allowing youth to connect with their environment while learning from Elders about their deep family and ancestral ties to the territory.\n\n-Intergenerational Community Events: Events like Family Fields Day and annual pow wows bring all ages together for shared experiences, laughter, and celebration, fostering connections and strengthening bonds across generations.\n\nFort William First Nation birch bark canoe building near Thunder Bay, Canada | Northern Ontario Travel \n\n-HelpAge Canada has supported a \"Connected Elders & Youth\" program in northern communities, which pairs youth with Elders to help the latter build digital literacy skills. While this specific program operates in Nunavut, it highlights a model of youth-elder interaction for reducing social isolation and promoting skill sharing. \nHelpAge Canada - Connected Elders and Youth - Healthy Aging CORE National",
