    "survivors": "Residential School Survivors Initiative: Moose Cree First Nation has a specific initiative focused on understanding the truth of what happened at the local Bishop Horden Hall Indian Residential School. This includes archival research, gathering testimonies, searching for unmarked burials, and memorialization activities, which are guided by survivors for healing.\n\nLand-Based Healing Program (Pim Pici Wak): This program offers culturally based activities focused on a holistic model of recovery, integrating Cree cultural values with Western practices.\nMoose Cree Survivors Initiative Opening Ceremony \n\n Land-Based Healing Program \n\nChristmas Dinner for Elders and Residential School Survivors",
    "youth": "Cultural & Land-Based Programs: Programs emphasize traditional knowledge, language revitalization, and connecting to the land for resilience and self-reliance, using resources like snow machines, boats, and wilderness camps.\n Project George: An Indigenous Land-Based Approach to Resilience for Youth",
