    "survivors": "Community-Led Healing: Funding goes to Indigenous organizations to design culturally relevant healing projects, including healing circles, ceremonies, and gatherings.\n\nTrauma-Informed Services: Programs provide counseling with Elders, culturally safe mental wellness services, and support networks for families and survivors.",
    "youth": "Education & Skills: Investment in new, safe schools to provide quality learning and skills for economic participation, plus programs like Jays Care Foundation for youth leadership and activities.\n\nJays Care Foundation has partnered with the government of Ontario to fund youth programs in Fort Severn First Nation, specifically through an investment in a local youth champion to lead activities and events in the community. \nJays Care Foundation | Toronto Blue Jays",
    "connect": "Organize regular gatherings where elders share Cree language, history, traditional skills (hunting, fishing, sewing), and life lessons, with youth recording and preserving these stories.\nYouth Learning From Elders: Understanding Who We Are - Centre for First Nations Governance\n\nYouth-Led Initiatives: Support youth councils to organize events, learn leadership, and advocate for elder involvement in schools, as described in\nInherent Rights Youth Initiative - Centre for First Nations Governance",
