    "link": "https://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/",
    "contact": "Address\n22 Mary Walk, Elliot Lake, ON P5A 1Z9, Canada\nEmail: ifc@niigaaniin.com\nContact:(705) 413-3854\nLink:https://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/",
    "physical": "Walk & Talk:\nWeekly walking group combining physical exercise with social support (hosted by Carly Dell).\nhttps://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/\n\n\n\nBowling Nights:\nActive recreation program held at the Northern Light Bowling Centre to encourage movement and community building.\n\nhttps://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/",
    "mental": "Youth Drop-In:\nSafe space for youth to gather, promoting social inclusion and mental well-being.\nhttps://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/\n\n\n\nLearn and Lounge:\nEducational and social sessions that reduce isolation and foster community connection.\nhttps://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/\n\n\n\nBook Club:\nGroup reading activities that support cognitive health and social interaction.\nhttps://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/",
    "spiritual": "",
    "emotional": "What is Working:        \n\nCommunity Wellness Initiatives: Provides a safe, inclusive space for Indigenous individuals to connect, offering wellness initiatives tailored to the unique needs of community members. \nLink:https://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/\n\n\nYouth Mentorship & Safe Spaces: Operates dedicated safe spaces and mentorship programs specifically for youth (ages 14-24), including Wednesday Youth Drop-Ins, Youth Boys Drum Night, and a Youth Council, which foster emotional regulation and peer support. \nLink:https://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/\nCultural Connection & \"Walk & Talk\": Promotes healing through ancestral wisdom and cultural connection. Programs like the \"Thursday Walk & Talk,\" \"Language Drop-in,\" and \"Craft Tuesdays\" provide low-barrier, culturally safe emotional support without the stigma of formal clinical therapy",
    "survivors": "Immediate Medical & Trauma Response:\nSafe Harbour / Drop-In Entry: While not an emergency medical clinic, the Friendship Centre acts as a primary, safe, low-barrier entry point for individuals and youth in distress to find immediate community connection and a supportive environment away from unsafe situations.\n\nCultural Safety & Empowerment: Support is rooted in a trauma-informed approach that honors ancestral wisdom, providing survivors a culturally safe space to regain a sense of identity, empowerment, and hope for positive change. \n\n\nDirect Social Work Advocacy:\n\nInformal Advocacy & Education: The dedicated Start-Up Coordinator and team lead programs that promote cultural awareness and community empowerment, offering direct, informal advocacy and daily emotional support to community members navigating complex challenges.\n\n\nThe \"Hub and Spoke\" Referral Model:\n\nNiigaaniin Services Connection: Operating in connection with Niigaaniin (the social services arm for the North Shore Tribal Council, as indicated by their direct ifc@niigaaniin.com contact), the Centre acts as a soft entry \"hub\" where individuals can be referred to broader Niigaaniin social, housing, or financial assistance programs.\n\nCommunity Navigation: Staff can help refer individuals who disclose domestic violence or severe trauma to appropriate specialized Indigenous mental wellness providers or regional health teams.",
    "youth": "Research Link sources:\nEnji-maawinjidiyaang Indigenous Friendship Centre \n\nEnji-maawinjidiyaang Indigenous Friendship Centre\n\n\nWasa-Nabin Program (Ages 13-18): A self-development program focusing on leadership, goal setting, and healthy lifestyle choices for urban Indigenous youth, providing one-to-one support and group activities.\n\nCultural & Wellness Activities: Youth engage in cultural camps, workshops, and traditional activities to build identity, connection to elders, and mental wellness.\n\nSupport Services: Offers advocacy, justice services, and mentorship to help youth navigate various challenges, with a focus on prevention and community-based alternatives.\n\nEducation & Employment: Programs such as the Alternative Secondary School Program and Apatisiwin assist in academic and career development.",
    "connect": "Research Link resources: \nEnji-maawinjidiyaang Indigenous Friendship Centre \n\nEnji-maawinjidiyaang Indigenous Friendship Centre \n\nCultural Mentorship and Sharing: Implement programs like the Kizhaay Anishinaabe Niin (I am a Kind Man) or similar initiatives that bring together male survivors and youth to learn traditional teachings and foster healthy relationships.\n\nIntergenerational Activities: Utilize the Friendship Centre for community events like potlucks and workshops that encourage informal interaction, allowing for natural mentorship to occur between Elders/survivors and younger community members.\n\nSupport and Healing Circles: Facilitate safe, structured spaces for sharing experiences of trauma and resilience, which helps youth understand history while assisting survivors in their healing journeys.\n\nYouth Leadership Development: Involve youth in planning, research, and advisory roles within the Friendship Centre to create initiatives that bridge generational gaps, ensuring they are actively involved in the healing process.\n\nCultural Knowledge Sharing: Leverage local knowledge keepers for storytelling and traditional workshops, which are vital for transferring cultural pride and resilience from survivors to the next generation.",
    "population": "",
    "populationNote": "",
    "additionalLinks": "Additional information:\n\nThe National Association of Friendship Centres (NAFC) acts at the federal/national level,\n\n the Ontario Federation of Indigenous Friendship Centres (OFIFC) operates at the provincial level, \n\nand individual Friendship Centres serve as local/community-level hubs. \n\nTogether, they form a \"Friendship Centre Movement\" providing, services to urban Indigenous people.",
    "strategicPlan": "Indigenous Friendship Centres in Canada are part of a national network governed and supported by the National Association of Friendship Centres (NAFC). The NAFC acts as the national body representing over 100 local Friendship Centres and 7 Provincial/Territorial Associations across the country, serving as the largest urban Indigenous service delivery infrastructure\n\nThe NAFC connects these local centers to create a unified movement focused on delivering culturally appropriate programs and services to urban Indigenous people.\n\nNAFC Strategic Plan 2020-2030",
    "agm": "Indigenous Friendship Centres in Canada are part of a national network governed and supported by the National Association of Friendship Centres (NAFC). The NAFC acts as the national body representing over 100 local Friendship Centres and 7 Provincial/Territorial Associations across the country, serving as the largest urban Indigenous service delivery infrastructure\n\nThe NAFC connects these local centers to create a unified movement focused on delivering culturally appropriate programs and services to urban Indigenous people.\n\n NAFC Annual Meeting",
    "financials": "The National Association of Friendship Centres Financial Statements.  \n\n2023- 2024 Apatisiwin Annual Report",
